---

Create a presentation deck — 16:9 artboards, 1920×1080 — for a university algorithms
course project. This is an assessed 10 minute presentation for SC2001 (Algorithm Design
and Analysis). The full write-up is in `report.md` in this folder; use it as the source of
truth for anything not spelled out below.

## Grading rubric this deck is judged against

- **Correctness — algorithm & implementation: 40 %.** The deck must show the actual algorithm
  and prove it works. Give pseudocode real estate; state the key-comparison counting
  convention explicitly; show the correctness evidence.
- **Analysis — theoretical & empirical: 40 %.** Both halves must be visible. Derived formulas
  *and* measured data, with the two shown agreeing.
- **Presentation & clarity of slides: 20 %.** This is your design brief.

A course constraint worth honouring: **code must be readable by someone unfamiliar with the
language.** Any code on a slide should be language-neutral pseudocode with plain-English
annotations — not raw C.

## Pacing — 10 minutes

19 content slides is more than 10 minutes allows (~30 s each). Build all of them, but mark the
following as **optional** in the speaker notes so they can be skipped live and kept for Q&A:
**4** (counting convention — the key points survive on slide 7), **18** (why libraries use 16–64),
and either **2** or **6**. That leaves a ~15-slide core at ~40 s each, which is realistic.
Slides **10, 11, 14 and 16** carry the most marks (the corrected optimum, the base-case crossover,
the S recommendation, and the memory-hierarchy explanation) — never cut those for time.

## Design direction

- Clean academic style, projector-legible: assume the back row is 12 m away. Body text no
  smaller than 28 px at 1920 width; slide titles 56–72 px. Never fill a slide with prose —
  target under ~40 words of body copy per slide.
- **One idea per slide.** Every analysis slide ends with a single bold one-line takeaway in a
  tinted callout strip along the bottom.
- **Match the palette to the embedded figures**, so the plots look native rather than pasted in.
  The figures use: hybrid = blue `#1f77b4`, original merge sort = red `#d62728`, insertion sort
  = orange `#ff7f0e`. Use blue as the primary accent and red as the contrast accent; keep the
  background near-white (`#fbfbfa` or similar) with near-black text.
- Number every slide, and carry a thin progress indicator or section label so the audience
  knows where they are in (a) → (b) → theory → (c) → (d) → conclusions.
- Formulas are the star of several slides: set display equations large and centred, with the
  meaning of each symbol labelled *beside* the term it belongs to (leader lines or coloured
  annotations), not in a separate legend. Do not use raw LaTeX source as text — render the
  math as styled HTML/SVG.

