# Slide-by-slide content

### 1 — Title
SC2001 Algorithm Design and Analysis · Project 1
**Integration of Merge Sort and Insertion Sort**
Subtitle: *When doing more comparisons makes you faster*
Space for team member names.

### 2 — The idea
Merge sort's recursion is wasteful on tiny subarrays: two function calls, a buffer write and a
copy-back per element. Insertion sort is cheap on small inputs. Set a threshold **S** — once a
subarray is ≤ S elements, switch.
Small diagram: a recursion tree whose bottom few levels are shaded and labelled "insertion
sort", the upper levels labelled "merge".
**Takeaway:** The question is not *whether* to switch, but *where*.

### 3 — (a) The algorithm
Pseudocode, language-neutral, with plain-English comments (indices inclusive both ends):

```
hybridSort(a, left, right, S):
    if left >= right: return                    # 0 or 1 element, nothing to do
    if right - left + 1 <= S:                   # subarray is small
        insertionSort(a, left, right); return   #   -> switch to insertion sort
    mid = left + (right - left) / 2             # integer division
    hybridSort(a, left,  mid,   S)              # sort left half
    hybridSort(a, mid+1, right, S)              # sort right half
    merge(a, left, mid, right)                  # combine the two sorted halves
```

Call out in a side box: **S = 1 makes this exactly the original merge sort** — the base case
only ever fires on single elements, where insertion sort does nothing. Every "S = 1" column in
this study is therefore both a baseline and a self-check.
**Takeaway:** One parameter, one extra branch — that is the entire modification.

### 4 — What counts as a key comparison
This has to be pinned down or none of the numbers mean anything.
- **Merge:** exactly one key comparison per iteration of the main `while (i ≤ mid and j ≤ right)`
  loop. The tail loops that copy the remainder of one run perform **none**.
- **Insertion sort:** the test `a[j] > key` is counted every time it is evaluated — **including
  the final failing one** that stops the scan. The bound test `j >= left` is an index test and
  is not counted.
- Index and bound tests are never key comparisons.
**Takeaway:** That one failing comparison is exactly what produces the −H<sub>m</sub> term later —
the convention is not cosmetic.

### 5 — (a) Correctness evidence
- `./hybrid_sort verify` sorts random arrays for **every n = 1…300** × **every S ∈ {1, 4, 7, 10,
  13, 16, 19}** and asserts non-decreasing output. All pass.
- Every experiment run re-checks sortedness before its result is recorded.
- An independent **Java** implementation with the same generator reproduces the C comparison
  counts **bit-for-bit** — a cross-check on the counting logic, not just the sorting.
- `S = 1` reproduces the pure merge-sort counts to the comparison.
- The whole suite was run on **two unrelated systems** (a 1-vCPU Ubuntu 24.04 VM with gcc 13.3, and
  the machine in slide 6). **All 585 key-comparison values came out bit-for-bit identical.**
**Takeaway:** Five independent checks, no failures.

### 6 — (b) Experimental setup
13 sizes from **1,000 to 10,000,000** · integers drawn uniformly from **[1, 10,000,000]** ·
xorshift64\* generator with fixed seeds, so both algorithms see byte-identical input ·
data generation and the sortedness check are **outside** the timed region · small inputs
repeated up to **50×** and the times averaged.
Machine: **Intel Core Ultra 7 265KF**, 31 GB RAM, Ubuntu 26.04 under WSL2, **gcc 15.2.0 -O2**,
CPU time via `clock()` at microsecond resolution.
**Takeaway:** Reproducible to the byte; timing measures sorting and nothing else.

### 7 — Theory I: the two cost formulas
Two boxed results, each with a one-line derivation sketch beside it.

**Merging two random sorted runs of length p and q:**
$$M(p,q) = p + q - \frac{p}{q+1} - \frac{q}{p+1}$$
*Why:* the loop stops as soon as one run empties and the other's tail is copied free. For any
element of A, the group {that element} ∪ B has q+1 members, and it is the largest with
probability 1/(q+1) — so p/(q+1) elements of A are expected to be copied free.

**Insertion sort on m random keys:**
$$C_{\text{ins}}(m) = \frac{m(m+3)}{4} - H_m$$
*Where H<sub>m</sub> comes from:* H<sub>m</sub> = 1 + ½ + ⅓ + … + 1/m is the **m-th harmonic
number** (≈ ln m + 0.577; H₁₀ = 2.93, H₂₀ = 3.60, H₄₀ = 4.28). Inserting element i costs
i/2 + 1 − 1/(i+1) on average: the "−1/(i+1)" is the case where the key is a **new minimum**, the
scan runs off the left end, and the final *failing* comparison never happens. Summing those
gives −H<sub>m</sub>.
Worst case m(m−1)/2, best case m−1.
**Takeaway:** H<sub>m</sub> is a small correction, not a mystery — it is the comparison that
doesn't happen.

### 8 — Theory II: where the 1.26 in *n* log₂*n* − 1.26*n* comes from
Build it in two steps.
1. A level of the tree merging runs of size s costs **n − n/(s+1)**, not n — slightly less,
   because merges finish early.
2. Summing over all log₂ n levels:
$$C_{\text{ms}}(n) = n\log_2 n - n\sum_{j\ge1}\frac{1}{2^{\,j-1}+1}$$
and that series converges fast:
$$\tfrac12+\tfrac13+\tfrac15+\tfrac19+\tfrac1{17}+\tfrac1{33}+\dots = \mathbf{1.2645\ldots}$$
Show the partial sums marching to 1.2645 (0.500 → 0.833 → 1.033 → 1.144 → 1.203 → 1.234 → …).
Verified: at n = 2²⁰, (n log₂n − C_ms)/n = **1.26450**.
Note that **more than half the constant (½ + ⅓ = 0.833) comes from the bottom two levels** —
which is exactly the part the hybrid deletes. Flag this; it pays off on slide 14.
**Takeaway:** 1.26 is a convergent series, not a fitted fudge factor.

### 9 — Theory III: S is not what the algorithm feels
The recursion halts after an **integer** number of halvings:
$$k=\left\lceil\log_2\frac{n}{S}\right\rceil,\qquad m=\frac{n}{2^{k}}\in\left(\tfrac{S}{2},\,S\right]$$
**m** — the *effective leaf size* — is what insertion sort actually receives, and it can be as
small as **half** of S. Two consequences, both visible in the data later:
1. Every S inside a plateau builds the **identical** recursion tree → identical comparison
   count. The count only changes when S crosses ⌈n/2ᵏ⌉.
2. With S fixed, **m oscillates as n grows.** At S = 16: m = 15.6 (n=10³), 9.8 (5·10³),
   12.2 (10⁵), 15.3 (10⁶), 9.5 (10⁷).
**Takeaway:** S is a knob with detents — the algorithm only sees ⌈log₂(n/S)⌉.

### 10 — Theory IV: closed form, and which S minimises comparisons
$$C(n,S)\;\approx\;\underbrace{n\log_2\tfrac{n}{m}-\tfrac{2n}{m}}_{\text{merging}}\;+\;\underbrace{\tfrac{n(m+3)}{4}-\tfrac{n H_m}{m}}_{\text{insertion at the leaves}}\;=\;\Theta\big(n\log(n/S)+nS\big)$$
Θ(n log n) for constant S; degrades to Θ(n²) only when S = Θ(n). Accurate to better than **0.15 %**
against the exact recurrence for S ≥ 8 — but **−4.4 %** at S = 1, so the optimum is settled on the
exact recurrence, *not* on this formula. State that out loud; it is the methodological point of the
section.

Then the answer, in two steps. Present as two columns:

| Step 1 — which leaf size m? | Step 2 — which S? |
|---|---|
| Exact recurrence at n = 2²⁰, exact integer H_m | S fixes depth k = ⌈log₂(n/S)⌉, which fixes m = n/2ᵏ |
| m = 1: **18.7355** · m = 2: **18.7355** · m = 4: 18.7980 | Both maps are non-decreasing ⇒ cost is non-decreasing in S |
| m = 8: 19.1791 · m = 16: 20.4187 · m = 32: 23.5619 | C(n,S+1) ≥ C(n,S) at **every** S = 1…511, **zero violations**, at five different n |
| Cost never falls as m grows ⇒ **m = 1 wins** | ⇒ **argmin S = {1, 2, 3}**, an exact three-way tie |

**Takeaway:** in key comparisons the best threshold is S ≤ 3 — and since S = 1 *is* pure merge sort,
the comparison-optimal hybrid is the one that never calls insertion sort at all. Stress that no
approximation was used anywhere in this argument: integers only.

*If time allows*, one cross-check: the closed form is smooth in m, so differentiate it.
f′(m) = −1/(m ln2) + 1/4 + (1+H_m)/m² stays positive for every m ≥ 1 — it comes down to **+0.08**
near m ≈ 3.5 and turns back up — so f has no interior minimum, the same answer. Put
`fig6b_slope_vs_leafsize.png` on the slide and let it carry the point: the curve never touches the
zero line.

### 11 — Base case: insertion sort never wins on comparisons
Embed `fig5_base_case.png` on the right. Table on the left:

| m | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 10 | 20 | 40 |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| C_ins(m) | 0 | 1 | 2.667 | 4.917 | 7.717 | 29.57 | 111.40 | 425.72 |
| C_ms(m) | 0 | 1 | 2.667 | 4.667 | 7.167 | 22.67 | 63.52 | 165.13 |
| difference | 0 | 0 | **0** | +0.25 | +0.55 | +6.90 | +47.89 | +260.59 |

Insertion sort is **exactly tied** for m ≤ 3 and strictly worse from m = 4 — it is *never*
cheaper. The dashed formula line sits on the measured points to within **0.12 %**, which
validates both the derivation and the counting convention.
**Takeaway:** No S can beat pure merge sort on comparisons — so any benefit must come from
cost *per operation*. This one slide predicts the whole result.

### 12 — (c)(i) Comparisons vs n, S fixed at 16
Embed `fig1a_comparisons_vs_n.png` and `fig1b_comparisons_per_element.png` side by side.
Compact table above or beside:

| n | hybrid (S=16) | model | merge sort | model |
|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| 1,000 | 10,342 | 10,324 | 8,700 | 8,707 |
| 100,000 | 1,639,740 | 1,639,403 | 1,536,422 | 1,536,367 |
| 10,000,000 | 226,416,986 | 226,417,506 | 220,102,645 | 220,100,699 |

- Measured within **0.17 %** of the exact model at all 13 sizes; within **0.012 %** for n ≥ 10⁶.
- Least-squares fit of the pure merge sort data: **C = 1.0032 n log₂n − 1.318 n** — recovering
  the 1.2645 constant of slide 8.
- **fig 1a** (log–log): straight lines of slope just above 1; the model line is invisible
  under the measured points. A log–log plot alone cannot separate n log n from n^1.03 — hence
  fig 1b.
- **fig 1b** is the sharper test: dividing by n turns Θ(n log n) into a **straight line against
  log₂n**. Red merge sort is straight and sits a constant ≈ 1.26 *below* the grey log₂n
  reference — the constant, read straight off the plot.
- Blue sits **above** red everywhere, and is **wavy, not straight** — that is m oscillating in
  (8, 16]. Excess by size: **+18.9 %** (n=10³, m=15.6), +5.9 % (5·10³, m=9.8), +6.7 % (10⁵),
  +8.3 % (10⁶), **+2.9 %** (10⁷, m=9.5).
**Takeaway:** Θ(n log n) confirmed to 0.17 %, and at fixed S the hybrid does 3–19 % *more*
comparisons — the wobble is the effective leaf size, not noise.

### 13 — (c)(ii) Comparisons vs S, n fixed
Embed `fig2a_comparisons_vs_S.png` and `fig2b_time_vs_S.png` side by side.

| S (n = 10⁶) | 1 | 4 | 8 | 16 | 32 | 64 | 128 | 192 | 256 |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| key comparisons (millions) | 18.67 | 18.73 | 19.07 | 20.23 | 23.18 | 29.89 | 44.22 | 44.22 | 73.83 |
| ↳ as a ratio, ÷ the 18.67 M at S=1 | 1.000 | 1.003 | 1.021 | 1.083 | 1.241 | 1.600 | 2.368 | 2.368 | 3.953 |
| CPU time (ms) | 64.6 | 60.8 | 58.2 | 57.3 | 56.7 | 55.6 | 54.4 | 53.8 | 57.5 |

The second row is a **ratio**, not a count: at S=256 the algorithm does 73.83 *million*
comparisons, which is 3.953 *times* the 18.67 million pure merge sort needs.

- **fig 2a — a staircase, not a curve**, and the model overlays it (max deviation 0.19 % at
  n=10⁵, 0.13 % at n=10⁶). Risers sit exactly at ⌈n/2ᵏ⌉: **S = 16, 31, 62, 123, 245** for
  n = 10⁶; **13, 25, 49, 98, 196** for n = 10⁵. They come in *pairs* ({15,16}, {30,31}, …)
  because 10⁶ is not a power of two, so subarrays at one depth differ in size by one.
- Worth calling out: **S = 128 and S = 192 give the identical 44.22 M comparisons** at n = 10⁶ —
  two thresholds 50 % apart, one identical algorithm.
- Each riser roughly **doubles m**, and the insertion term doubles with it: at n = 10⁷ the
  insertion component runs 14.8 M → 28.4 M → 53.3 M → 101.8 M → 197.6 M → 388.6 M for
  S = 8, 16, 32, 64, 128, 256 (×1.9 per riser). Totals grow more slowly — +0.5 %, +2.9 %,
  +10.1 %, +27.8 %, +66.9 %, +149.2 % — because each riser also removes a whole merge level ≈ n.
- **fig 2b — CPU time behaves completely differently:** a steep drop to S ≈ 30, then a long
  shallow floor, turning back up past S ≈ 200. Minima at **S = 66, 84, 141, 88, 142** for
  n = 10³…10⁷ — an order of magnitude above the thresholds real implementations use.
- **The hybrid's relative advantage shrinks as n grows** — visible only now that fig 2b covers five
  sizes. Time at the best S, vs S=1: −34.4 % (10³), −26.1 % (10⁴), −17.8 % (10⁵), −17.4 % (10⁶),
  −13.6 % (10⁷). The hybrid deletes a *fixed* number of merge levels while the tree has log₂n of
  them, so the fraction removed falls as n rises.
- The darkest-purple n=10³ curve moves in visible quantisation steps (one sort ≈ 21 µs); its shape
  is meaningful, its points are not.
**Takeaway:** The two figures disagree about what S should be — and that disagreement is the
whole project.

### 14 — (c)(iii) Choosing S
Embed `fig3a_comparison_cost_vs_S.png` and `fig3b_time_vs_S_optimal.png` side by side. If any
figure in the deck earns a full slide of its own, it is **fig 3b** — it carries the headline answer.

Sweep runs to **S = 512**. Say why in one line: an earlier sweep stopped at 128 and found its
"optimum" sitting on that boundary, which measures where the search stopped, not where the
optimum is. The range was extended until the curve turned up on both sides.

| n | best S by comparisons | best S by CPU | time at best vs S=1 | within 2 % of best |
|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| 1,000 | 1–3 | 80 | −33 % | 80–224 |
| 10,000 | 1–3 | 80 | −27 % | 80–128 |
| 100,000 | 1–3 | 128 | −23 % | 112–192 |
| 1,000,000 | 1–3 | 192 | −16 % | 64–224 |
| 10,000,000 | 1–3 | 80 | −15 % | 80–224 |

- **fig 3b** is a clear **U**: steep drop to S ≈ 10, long shallow floor, sharp rise past S ≈ 256.
  The per-n optima (★) scatter across S = 80–192 with no meaningful single winner — and three of
  the five sizes share S = 80, so their stars overlap (each curve's best S is in the legend).
  **The shape is the result, not the star.**
- Comparison flatness depends on n — the common claim "S = 1…8 is all equivalent" is true at
  10⁷ but **not** at 10³. Within 0.5 % of the minimum: S = 1–3 (n=10³), 1–4, 1–5, 1–6, 1–8 (10⁷).
- So pick the S that is never far from best at *any* size — worst-case time across all five:

| S | 16 | 32 | 64 | 80 | 96 | 112 | **128** | 160 | 192 | 256 |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| worst-case time vs best | +19.2 % | +9.6 % | +6.4 % | +3.8 % | +3.9 % | +0.9 % | **+0.6 %** | +3.2 % | +4.5 % | +18.2 % |
| excess comparisons @ 10⁷ | +2.9 % | +10.1 % | +27.8 % | +66.9 % | +66.9 % | +66.9 % | **+66.9 %** | +149.2 % | +149.2 % | +149.2 % |

**Recommendation: S ≈ 128** — within **0.6 %** of best time at every size tested. Note S = 80, 96
and 128 build the *identical* tree at n = 10⁷ (m = 76.3), so they differ only by timing noise.
Anything in **S ∈ [80, 192]** is defensible. **If comparisons carry weight, use S ≈ 64**: it gives
up ~2.6 % of the time saving and more than halves the comparison penalty (+27.8 % vs +66.9 %).
**Takeaway:** Two metrics, two different optima. Comparisons say S ≤ 3; CPU time says S ≈ 128 —
an order of magnitude above the conventional answer.

