### 16 — (d) Why: the accounting
The resolution of the paradox — a table with the mechanism spelled out.

| at n = 10⁷ | pure merge sort | hybrid (S = 128) | change |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| subarrays given to insertion sort | 10,000,000 (size 1) | **131,072** (size **76.29**) | — |
| merge levels executed | 23.32 | 17.00 | −6.32 |
| merge comparisons | 220,100,699 | 169,740,116 | **−50.4 M** |
| insertion comparisons | 0 | 197,597,009 | **+197.6 M** |
| **total comparisons** | **220,100,699** | **367,337,124** | **+147.2 M (+66.9 %)** |
| recursive calls | 19,999,999 | 262,143 | **−19.7 M** |
| element writes (buffer + copy back) | 466,445,568 | 340,000,000 | **−126.4 M** |

Two bullets — the second is the real insight, give it weight:
- **Comparisons lost:** cutting 6.32 merge levels saves 50.4 M but costs 197.6 M in insertion
  sort. Worse, by slide 8 the deleted levels are the *cheapest* ones — over half the 1.26n
  discount lives in the bottom two levels. The hybrid gives up merge sort's best-value work.
- **Time won — because not all comparisons cost the same.** At n = 10⁷ the array is **40 MB**,
  far beyond any cache, so every merge level is a streaming pass through **DRAM**: each
  comparison drags a cache line with it. A leaf of 76 `int`s is **304 bytes** and lives entirely
  in **L1**, so insertion sort's ~20 comparisons per element cost about a cycle each, in place,
  with no scratch buffer and no copy-back. The trade is ~6.3 memory-bound merge levels for ~20
  cache-resident comparisons per element — and it pays: 126 M fewer memory writes (27 % less
  merge traffic) and a recursive-call count that collapses by a factor of **76**.
If the design allows one diagram, draw the memory hierarchy: a 40 MB array streaming from DRAM
per merge level, versus a 304-byte leaf sitting inside L1.
**Takeaway:** The win is a constant factor — fewer passes over DRAM, fewer calls, comparisons paid
out of L1 — not an asymptotic one. Both algorithms remain Θ(n log n).

### 17 — Conclusions
1. **Θ(n log(n/S) + nS)** comparisons; Θ(n log n) for constant S. The exact average-case model
   matches every measurement to **0.17 %** (0.012 % for n ≥ 10⁶).
2. Fixed S → n log n growth (fig 1); fixed n → a **staircase in S** with risers at ⌈n/2ᵏ⌉,
   because S enters only through the integer depth ⌈log₂(n/S)⌉ (fig 2).
3. **Comparisons are the wrong objective for tuning S** — insertion sort ties merge sort for
   m ≤ 3 and loses from m = 4, so the count is minimised at S ≤ 3 and rises thereafter. The
   corrected continuous analysis predicts exactly this.
4. **CPU time is the right objective and its optimum is far above convention. S ≈ 128 recommended**
   — within **0.6 %** of best at every size tested. S ≈ 64 if comparisons carry weight in the
   cost model (+27.8 % rather than +66.9 %, for ~2.6 % more time).
5. On 10 M integers the hybrid performs **66.9 % more comparisons** yet takes **14.1 % less CPU
   time** — and the speed-up rises *monotonically* with the comparison count across
   S = 20 → 128.
6. **Comparison counts are hardware-independent; the optimal S is not.** The same suite on two
   unrelated systems gave bit-for-bit identical counts, while the recommended threshold moved
   from S ≈ 32 on a 1-vCPU cloud VM to S ≈ 128 here. Any claim of "the" optimal S is a claim
   about a particular machine.

### 18 — Why libraries still use 16–64
Worth one slide — it pre-empts the obvious question and shows judgement. Timsort builds runs of
32–64; libstdc++'s introsort switches at 16. Our measured optimum is 128. Not a contradiction:
1. **Library sorts compare arbitrary types through a comparator.** Comparing two `int`s is one
   instruction; a string compare or user lambda is far more. Once comparisons get expensive the
   Θ(nS) term bites sooner and the optimum slides back to 16–32. We sort the cheapest possible key.
2. **This merge is deliberately the plain one** — into a scratch buffer, then `memcpy` back, so
   2n writes per level. A tuned sort ping-pongs between buffers and skips the copy-back, halving
   the per-level cost and lowering the optimal S.
3. **Libraries must be robust across input distributions**, not tuned to uniform random data.
**Takeaway:** The optimal S is a function of comparison cost, merge implementation and memory
hierarchy — this project fixes all three at one setting.

### 19 — Limitations
- **The optimal S is a property of the machine — demonstrated, not assumed.** The same suite on a
  1-vCPU Ubuntu 24.04 VM (gcc 13.3) put the basin at S ≈ 32; on the Core Ultra 7 it sits at
  S ≈ 128, a **4× shift**. Shape robust, centre not.
- Uniformly random data only. On nearly-sorted input insertion sort goes near-linear and the
  optimal S would be far larger — precisely the observation Timsort is built on.
- Values drawn from [1, 10⁷] with n up to 10⁷, so the largest datasets contain many duplicates
  while the model assumes distinct keys. Measured agreement (≤ 0.17 %) shows the effect is
  negligible here.
- Java is included to show the algorithm is not language-specific — its comparison counts match
  C exactly, but its timings are JIT-dominated and are not used.

